What is cgpaint
===============

cgpaint is a paint program, as the Paint of Windows 98 was: a picture
of so many points by so many, a box of sixteen tools at its left, a
box of colours beneath it, and a line at the bottom that says what the
tool is for and where the pointer is. It is written for CGTK and comes
from nowhere else.

The tools are Paint's, in Paint's order: the free-form and the
rectangular selection, the eraser, fill, pick color, the magnifier,
the pencil, the brush, the airbrush, text, the line, the curve, the
rectangle, the polygon, the ellipse and the rounded rectangle. The box
beneath them holds what the chosen one can be told: an eraser's size,
a brush's shape, a line's width, whether a shape is its edge, its edge
and its inside, or all of one colour, and whether what is selected is
laid down with its background or without.

The left button draws in the foreground's colour and the right one in
the background's, and each takes its colour from the colour box in the
same way. Twice on a colour of the box changes it.

The pointers that have a point to them are cgpaint's own and not the
desktop's: the pencil draws at its tip, the bucket fills where its
paint ends, the dropper takes the colour at its tip, the glass and
the can work about their middles, and everything else draws at the
gap in the middle of a cross. A cursor theme need not have a pencil,
and where it has one, where it draws is the theme's to say.

It reads and writes three kinds of file: Windows bitmaps (.bmp), XPM
(.xpm) and PNG (.png). How a name ends says which is written, and a
name that does not say is given .bmp. A bitmap is written with 24 bits
to a point; those of 1, 4, 8, 24 and 32 bits are read, if they are not
packed. Nothing in a picture is seen through: what a PNG or an XPM
file lets through is white.

What is copied goes to the clipboard as a PNG file, so it can be
pasted into other programs, and a picture copied in another program
can be pasted here.

Undo goes back as far as fifty steps, where Paint went three; fewer
for a very large picture, as the earlier pictures are kept whole, in
128 megabytes at most, and three at the least.


What it has not
===============

Paint's printing, Set As Wallpaper, View Bitmap and thumbnail are not
here, nor its bitmaps of 2, 16 and 256 colours. What is selected has
no handles to stretch it by: Image > Stretch and Skew does that, by
numbers. Text is typed at its end only, and its font is chosen in View
> Text Font and not in a bar of its own. The two items of the Image
menu are "Flip and Rotate" and "Stretch and Skew", as a menu of the
toolkit's cannot have a stroke in an item's name.

It is in seven languages: German, Spanish, French, Japanese, Brazilian
Portuguese, Russian and Simplified Chinese. The names of the tools and
of the menus are those Paint had in each. The translations were made
by machine and have not been reviewed by native speakers.


Building
========

See the README at the top of cgtools. cgpaint needs libpng, 1.6 or
later, which CGTK needs too; configure looks for it.


Use
===

  cgpaint [FILE]

  Shift             with a line, keeps it level, upright or at half a
                    right angle; with a shape, makes it as wide as high
  the other button  while one is down, undoes what that one was doing
  Escape            the same; or ends the selection, laying it down
  Delete            clears what is selected
  Control with a selection dragged leaves a copy of it behind

The man page, cgpaint(1), has the rest.
